If you have the ability to pilot these setups you probably don't need a guide but I figured I would share what is possible. These setups are more common in small teams (2-4man at 510-555 invo) where you can drop a few items that can be shared between teammates/are not required by everyone such as [[bone dagger]], [[black chinchompa]], or even [[divine super combat]], but you can definitely use these setups in a solo. You can also bring [[torva platelegs]] and switch to [[aranea boots]] which gives a more pleasant switching experience as well as additional ranged accuracy. '''Don't use aranea boots without torva legs.'''

These setups basically require you to go power->life. Of course it's possible to clear without going life second but I don't think that amount of supplies is worth the effort needed to consistently clear with that few supplies.

The time save in the 8way range setup is obviously at [[Zebak]], and the ranging portions of [[Akkha]] and Wardens P2, and definitely more consistent than a [[tumeken's shadow]] Zebak setup.

The [[Spellbook swap]] setup is a more experimental one, that allows you to cast [[Vengeance]] for additional damage at [[Ba-ba]] (assuming 5:1 which you should be doing at this point) and P2 [[Elidinis' Warden]] for free, as well as opportunities to take vengs in all the other rooms if you want to. It also allows for unique strategies using the [[Spellbook swap]] as a stall in [[Kephri]] to trap both of the [[Spitting Scarab]] without tanking any damage. I would highly recommend having at least the Master [[combat achievements]] completed if not Grandmaster if you want to try this setup. You have opportunities to cast [[Spellbook swap]] without losing DPS in every single room, but it can be challenging to do without making mistakes especially in [[Akkha]] and P3/P4 [[Tumeken's Warden]].

'''Notes on items:'''
* [[Avernic treads]]: Though it is only one inventory slot, this potential item will likely make 8way range setups more common (and easier) to run.
* [[Dorgeshuun crossbow]]: Due to a bug with [[bone dagger]], you can only spec [[Kephri]] during a small window following a swarming phase in a solo. There is also a one tick window as you get dunged that you can also get a bone spec off. That is not possible to do with a bone dagger (unless you get creative with stalls+dung, but I am not currently aware of any setup that allows you to get this off on the first dung). This is however possible to do with a [[dorgeshuun crossbow]]. With that said, this weapon is also currently bugged and I have no idea if/when it will be fixed. The viability of this is also questionable as it has a lower max hit than [[bone dagger]], and you would likely need to use this on [[Ba-ba]] as I would not bring two bone weapons. If you are in a team raid bone dagger will work as long as you use it after someone has just hit Kephri.

==What if I don't have max gear?==

If you do not have at minimum a [[tumeken's shadow]], you will need a completely different guide which I will not go into today. If you do have at least that though, your priority in terms of acquiring new gear should be,

'''GEAR THAT YOU NEED TO 3 DOWN WARDENS P2 (solo) > MAGIC DAMAGE GEAR >>> RANGED GEAR >= MELEE GEAR'''

"Gear that you need to 3 down wardens" can become quite relevant as you turn on invocations such as Overly Draining, Dehydration, and just invocation number in general. You can make use of [[dragon dagger]] to make up for a lack of gear for awhile, but as you really push invocation you will need to start acquiring better gear. It is extremely important that you do not 4 down Warden P2, as this is massive time loss that rewards no additional points. If you are doing a team raid, this is much less important and you can just go crazy with a [[dragon dagger]]. An [[abyssal tentacle]] is a good budget core hitting weapon prior to a [[ghrazi rapier]] if Overly Draining is on or if your team is 3 downing anyway.

After 3 downing, your main priority should be obtaining as much magic % damage gear as possible. I would keep only the core melee and ranged items such as [[osmunten's fang]], [[amulet of torture]], [[ferocious gloves]], [[necklace of anguish]], and [[toxic blowpipe]], and go full focus on obtaining magic % damage starting from [[bloodbark]] all the way up to [[ancestral]], acquiring solid value pieces along the way such as [[ancestral hat]], and [[magus ring]]. Given that your best DPS on [[Zebak]] will likely be shadow, I do think it is worth it to acquire armor such as [[Ahrim's]] or [[blue moon armor]] over [[bloodbark]] even though that does not result in any additional max hits. It may be worth it to obtain a special attack weapon such as [[Voidwaker]] along the way. [[Burning claws]] are a solid budget alternative which can even be superior depending on the boss. It is worth noting that [[burning claws]] are pretty bad on [[Tumeken's warden]] with no defence reduction.

After max mage, I would focus on reobtaining special attack weapons like [[voidwaker]] or [[zaryte crossbow]], obtaining unfortified [[masori armor]], and an [[amulet of rancour]].